.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 103 PM PST Sat Nov 14 2026
(This evening through Sunday night)

In wake of yesterday`s frontal passage, isolated to scattered
rain showers are forecast to persist across the North Bay and
parts of the Bay Area through early evening as many of the CAMs
(Convection- Allowing Models) are indicating them to persist.
Otherwise, expecting a mix of sun and clouds throughout the day
with temperatures slightly below seasonal averages.

This is all ahead of our next cold front that is expected to move
across the North Bay between 8 AM and 4 PM Sunday, the Bay Area
and the Santa Cruz Mountains between noon and 8 PM, and the
Central Coast between 4 PM and midnight Monday morning. There are
subtle differences in the timing of the front`s arrival, resulting
in broader windows of expected arrival times. Southerly winds are
forecast to increase ahead of the front, although they are not
expected to be as strong as with the previous system. The
potential for thunderstorms is also lower.

Rain totals will range from 0.50"-1.0" across most of the Bay
Area, Santa Cruz County, and the Santa Lucia range, 1.0"-1.5" in
the Sonoma County coastal ranges, and 0.25"-0.50" across the
interior portions of the East Bay and the Central Coast. Winds
will be elevated, but will not be as strong as Friday`s system
with southerly gusts of 20 to 30 mph possible across the SF Bay
Area, up to 45 mph along the coast and in the higher elevations.
In wake of the frontal passage, expecting winds to shift out of
the northwest and ease.

See the BEACHES section for more details, but to quote: "These waves
bring tremendous power that will overpower anyone in the water and
cause coastal erosion. The risk of drowning is very high this
weekend. Do not go in the water, avoid climbing on the rocks or
leaning over seawalls, and never turn your back on the ocean."

